    "definition_tex": "\\label{problem:OP-0208}\n% GAME_THEORY_PROBLEM: {\"local_id\": \"GT-078\", \"original_number\": 78, \"kind\": \"R\", \"entry_kind\": \"statement_only\", \"published\": false, \"review_required\": true, \"category\": \"game_theory\"}\n\\label{game:GT-078}\n{\\sffamily\\small\\color{muted}\n{\\small\\textbf{GT-078} | Mean-field games | R: precise instantiation of a published question; review required\\par}\n\\par}\n\\subsection*{Definitions and mathematical statement}\nLet $\\mathbb T^d=\\mathbb R^d/\\mathbb Z^d$ have normalized Lebesgue measure, and let $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb T^d)$ denote its Borel probability measures. Fix couplings $F,G:\\mathbb T^d\\times\\mathcal P(\\mathbb T^d)\\to\\mathbb R$ that are smooth and Lasry--Lions monotone:\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb T^d}[K(x,m)-K(x,m')](m-m')(dx)\\geq0,\n \\qquad K\\in\\{F,G\\},\\quad m,m'\\in\\mathcal P(\\mathbb T^d).\n\\]\nFor a concrete smooth class, require continuous flat functional derivatives through order four, jointly $C^6$ with bounded spatial derivatives in all their spatial arguments. A first flat derivative $\\delta K/\\delta m$ means\n\\[\n \\left.\\frac d{d\\theta}K(x,(1-\\theta)m+\\theta m')\\right|_{\\theta=0^+}\n =\\int\\frac{\\delta K}{\\delta m}(x,m,y)(m'-m)(dy);\n\\]\nhigher derivatives are defined recursively. Assume the classical solutions specified below exist for each horizon $T>0$.\n\nFor $N\\geq2$, $\\boldsymbol x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$ and $m^{N,i}_{\\boldsymbol x}=(N-1)^{-1}\\sum_{j\\ne i}\\delta_{x_j}$, let $v^{N,T,i}$ solve the no-common-noise Nash system\n\\[\n \\begin{split}\n -\\partial_t v^{N,T,i}-\\sum_{j=1}^N\\Delta_{x_j}v^{N,T,i}\n +\\tfrac12|D_{x_i}v^{N,T,i}|^2\n +\\sum_{j\\ne i}D_{x_j}v^{N,T,i}\\cdot D_{x_j}v^{N,T,j}\n &=F(x_i,m^{N,i}_{\\boldsymbol x}),\\\\\n v^{N,T,i}(T,\\boldsymbol x)&=G(x_i,m^{N,i}_{\\boldsymbol x}).\n \\end{split}\n\\]\nDefine $U^T(t,x,m)$ as the representative player's value in the unique monotone MFG equilibrium on $[t,T]$ with population law $m$ at time $t$. Its optimal-control dynamics are $dX_s=\\alpha_s ds+\\sqrt2\\,dB_s$, and its cost is\n\\[\n \\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\int_t^T\\left(\\tfrac12|\\alpha_s|^2+F(X_s,m_s)\\right)ds\n +G(X_T,m_T)\\right],\\qquad X_t=x.\n\\]\nHere $(m_s)$ is the equilibrium population flow starting from $m$, kept fixed in the representative player's optimization. This characteristic definition specifies the master-equation solution and its terminal condition $U^T(T,x,m)=G(x,m)$.\n\nFor a function $f(\\boldsymbol x)$ set\n\\[\n \\mathcal C_i f(\\boldsymbol x)=f(\\boldsymbol x)-\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}f(x_1,\\ldots,z,\\ldots,x_N)dz,\n \\quad \\mathcal C U^T(t,x,m)=U^T(t,x,m)-\\int U^T(t,z,m)dz.\n\\]\nThe following norm choice is editorial. Determine whether\n\\[\n \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\sup_{T>0}\\max_{1\\leq i\\leq N}\n \\sup_{(t,\\boldsymbol x)\\in[0,T]\\times(\\mathbb T^d)^N}\n \\begin{aligned}[t]\n \\big(&|\\mathcal C_i v^{N,T,i}(t,\\boldsymbol x)\n       -\\mathcal C U^T(t,x_i,m^{N,i}_{\\boldsymbol x})|\\\\[-2pt]\n &+|D_{x_i}v^{N,T,i}(t,\\boldsymbol x)\n       -D_xU^T(t,x_i,m^{N,i}_{\\boldsymbol x})|\\big)=0.\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIf these assumptions do not suffice, identify the additional quantitative stability hypothesis needed for this estimate.\n\\subsection*{Short English statement}\nCan the finite-player feedbacks and centered values approximate the MFG values with an error independent of the time horizon?\n\\subsection*{Variants and scope boundaries}\nAn uncentered value estimate uniform over all horizons is a stronger question: a small error in an ergodic cost may accumulate linearly in $T$. Convergence of empirical trajectories, estimates only away from terminal time, and a common-noise version have different quantifiers and norms.\n\\subsection*{Source relationship and status}\nThe source asks for convergence uniform in time; the centered norm and smooth class here are editorial specifications. Its source-date open status is not an exhaustive 2026 literature certificate.\n\\subsection*{References}\n\\begingroup\\footnotesize\\raggedright\n{\\footnotesize\\raggedright\n\\textbf{[S1]} Pierre Cardaliaguet and Fran\\c{c}ois Delarue (2023). \\emph{Selected topics in mean field games}. Proceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians 2022, vol.~5, pp.~3660--3703, EMS Press; DOI: 10.4171/ICM2022/17. The volume was published on 15 December 2023; the chapter carries 2022 copyright.\\par\n\\textit{Locators:} Nash system (1.1), p.~3663; master equation (2.3); Section~3.2, final paragraph, p.~3680, uniform-time convergence question.\\par\n\\url{https://ems.press/content/book-chapter-files/33265}\\par}\n\\par\\endgroup\n\n\\subsection*{Common conventions: Source mathematical conventions}\n\n\\textbf{Finite games.} For a finite set $A$, $\\Delta(A)$ is its probability\nsimplex. Players choose independent mixed strategies in Nash equilibrium;\ncorrelation is allowed only when explicitly part of the solution concept.\nIn a game with payoff functions $u_i$ and strategy profile $x$, an additive\n$\\varepsilon$ Nash equilibrium satisfies\n\\[\n u_i(x)\\geq u_i(x_i',x_{-i})-\\varepsilon\n \\quad\\text{for every player $i$ and every admissible deviation $x_i'$}.\n\\]\nFor numerical approximation, payoffs are normalized as locally specified.\nAn $\\varepsilon$ payoff residual is distinct from distance at most\n$\\varepsilon$ to the set of exact equilibria. Point convergence, convergence\nof empirical play, and convergence in distance to an equilibrium set are\nalso distinct.\n\n\\textbf{Computational representations.} Unless stated otherwise, a complexity\nquestion takes rational data with binary encoding; polynomial time is measured\nin total bit length. A fixed-accuracy polynomial algorithm is not necessarily\npolynomial in $1/\\varepsilon$, and neither is necessarily polynomial in\n$\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$. Succinct games and value, demand, or sampling oracles\nmust declare their interfaces. Exact real solutions require an explicit\nrepresentation; an unspecified list of arbitrary real numbers is not a\nfinite computational output. A claimed algorithmic barrier is meaningful\nonly for its specified model and reductions.\n\n\\textbf{Dynamic games.} Histories, information, observation, and allowable\nrandomization are part of the model. A stationary strategy depends only on\nthe current state; a positional strategy in a deterministic graph game\nchooses one action at each controlled vertex. Nash equilibrium at an initial\nstate and equilibrium after every history are different requirements.\nAn expectation of a pathwise $\\liminf$ payoff, a $\\liminf$ of expected averages,\na limiting expected average, and a uniform finite-horizon equilibrium are\ndifferent criteria. None is silently substituted for another.\n\n\\textbf{Allocation and mechanisms.} A complete allocation assigns every item\nexactly once unless otherwise stated. Zero-valued goods, negative values,\ncapacity constraints, feasibility, lotteries, and copies of goods affect\nfairness definitions and are specified locally. Dominant-strategy incentive\ncompatibility, Bayesian incentive compatibility, universal truthfulness,\nand truthfulness in expectation impose different inequalities. Whenever\nan objective ratio has zero denominator, its convention is stated or those\ninstances are excluded.\n\n\\textbf{Infinite-dimensional models.} Expectations, integrals, stochastic\nequations, and optimization objectives require their local regularity,\nintegrability, filtrations, and admissible domains. A backward--forward\nmean-field system is a boundary-value problem; it is not an initial-value\nsemiflow without an additional construction. Long-time, large-population,\nand vanishing-noise limits require an order of limits and a convergence\ntopology. If a minimum need not be attained, the objective is its infimum\nor supremum and the approximation requirement is stated separately.",
    "definitionFile": "attacks/open_problems/economics/statements/C73-41.tex",
    "definition_file": "attacks/open_problems/economics/statements/C73-41.tex",
    "statement_url": "data/economics/statements/C73-41.tex",
    "entry_kind": "statement_only",
